issue_list.body:
## Summary

An Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) vulnerability exists in `xxl-job-admin/joblog/logDetailCat`. Any authenticated user who can obtain or guess a valid `logId` can read execution log content belonging to job groups they are not authorized to access.

Unlike the adjacent log detail page, the JSON log-reading endpoint does not enforce job-group authorization before returning log content. This leads to unauthorized disclosure of job execution logs, which may contain sensitive business parameters, internal network addresses, stack traces, operational metadata, and secrets written by jobs during execution.

## Vulnerability Description

The XXL-JOB admin console uses group-based authorization to restrict users to specific job groups. In the normal workflow, the log detail page checks whether the current user has permission for the corresponding `jobGroup`.

However, the endpoint that actually returns the log content, `logDetailCat`, does not perform the same authorization check. It only accepts a `logId`, loads the corresponding log record, and then fetches the remote log content from the executor.

As a result, any authenticated user can directly request log content for logs outside their authorized group scope if they know or can enumerate a valid `logId`.

### Affected Code

The page endpoint enforces authorization:

```java
// xxl-job-admin/src/main/java/com/xxl/job/admin/controller/biz/JobLogController.java
@RequestMapping("/logDetailPage")
public String logDetailPage(HttpServletRequest request, @RequestParam("id") long id, Model model){
    XxlJobLog jobLog = xxlJobLogMapper.load(id);
    if (jobLog == null) {
        throw new RuntimeException(I18nUtil.getString("joblog_logid_unvalid"));
    }

    // valid permission
    JobGroupPermissionUtil.validJobGroupPermission(request, jobLog.getJobGroup());
    ...
}
```

The JSON endpoint does not:

```java
// xxl-job-admin/src/main/java/com/xxl/job/admin/controller/biz/JobLogController.java
@RequestMapping("/logDetailCat")
@ResponseBody
public Response<LogResult> logDetailCat(@RequestParam("logId") long logId,
                                        @RequestParam("fromLineNum") int fromLineNum){
    try {
        XxlJobLog jobLog = xxlJobLogMapper.load(logId);
        if (jobLog == null) {
            return Response.ofFail(I18nUtil.getString("joblog_logid_unvalid"));
        }

        ExecutorBiz executorBiz = XxlJobAdminBootstrap.getExecutorBiz(jobLog.getExecutorAddress());
        Response<LogResult> logResult =
                executorBiz.log(new LogRequest(jobLog.getTriggerTime().getTime(), logId, fromLineNum));
        return logResult;
    } catch (Exception e) {
        logger.error(e.getMessage(), e);
        return Response.ofFail(e.getMessage());
    }
}
```

## Exploitation

### Preconditions

- The attacker has a valid authenticated account.
- The attacker does not need administrator privileges.
- The attacker only needs a valid `logId`, which may be obtained by normal usage, error messages, enumeration, or inference.

### Reproduction Steps

1. Create a new normal user u_g2 and grant it permissions only for the AI Executor Sample (xxl-job-executor-sample-ai)

<img width="1444" height="560" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a2bde7db-db5c-44a8-9246-8704916d254b" />

2. Then, use the administrator account to trigger a task for the General Executor Sample (xxl-job-executor-sample) and view the dispatch log as shown in the image below

<img width="1448" height="642" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/64b30645-af76-48c1-ace8-94b771863752" />

3. Click on the Execution Log to view the details.

<img width="1447" height="502" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/10cd92e8-3205-42cf-95ce-0d889713528d" />

4. Next, log in with the u_g2 account; you will see that the dispatch log list is empty.

<img width="1472" height="1145" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d9210cde-b0c1-4f36-8dc9-b1795c9d06e5" />

5. Attempting to call the logDetailPage interface using the u_g2 identity credential (cookie) will result in an error due to insufficient permissions.

<img width="1095" height="778" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/37e04987-5b53-464e-8040-7ba492ffba58" />

6. However, calling the logDetailCat interface successfully returns the log details, confirming a successful unauthorized log access (Broken Access Control).

<img width="1109" height="768" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/11de7a26-f892-43cb-8a55-eb5d885cb1bc" />

## Impact

- Unauthorized access to execution logs across job-group boundaries.
- Disclosure of internal hostnames, stack traces, job parameters, request/response data, and operational metadata.
- Possible leakage of credentials or tokens if jobs print secrets into logs.
- Increased attack surface because log data can reveal internal infrastructure and downstream systems.

## Remediation

- Apply `JobGroupPermissionUtil.validJobGroupPermission(...)` inside `logDetailCat` after loading the `XxlJobLog`.
- Treat `logId` as a protected object reference and verify that the current user is authorized to access the associated `jobGroup`.
- Avoid exposing predictable identifiers when possible.
- Add regression tests to ensure both `logDetailPage` and `logDetailCat` enforce identical authorization rules.

## Disclosure Notes

- This draft follows a public issue / CVE-style structure.
- The version range in the title is a disclosure placeholder and should be adjusted if the maintainer confirms a different affected range.

