I had a `caca-test` failure with GCC trunk:
```
caca_resize (cv=<optimized out>, width=<optimized out>, height=<optimized out>) at /var/tmp/portage/media-libs/libcaca-0.99_beta20/work/libcaca-0.99.beta20/caca/canvas.c:484
484                     chars[old_height * width + x] = (uint32_t)' ';
(gdb) p new_size
$1 = -1073741827
```

This comes from:
```
/* Check for overflow */
    int new_size = width * height;
    if (new_size < 0 || (width > 0 && new_size / width != height))
    {
        seterrno(EOVERFLOW);
        return -1;
    }
```

You need to check for the possibility of overflow first before doing the multiplication to avoid UB.

events.body:
I confirm that this is undefined behavior (due to overflow of _signed_ multiplication). I believe one way to do this with defined behavior would be:

```c
    if ((width != 0) && (INT_MAX / width < height)) {
        seterrno(EOVERFLOW);
        return -1;    
    }
    int new_size = width * height;
```

I can provide a pull request after #85 is clear, as needed.

I can confirm that this can cause problems and should be fixed. I put libcaca in a decompiler, and this is what it gets compiled to:
```c
  new_size = width * height;
  if ( ((width * height) & 0x80000000) != 0 )
  {
    *__errno_location() = 0x4B;
    return 0xFFFFFFFFLL;
  }
```

later when calling caca_add_dirty_rect and then merge_new_rect, this can cause
```c
        best_score = cv->width * cv->height;
```

to become zero due to integer overflow, which then causes
```c
    if(best_score > 0 && cv->ndirty < MAX_DIRTY_COUNT)
        return;
```
to not return. this then makes 
```c
    cv->dirty[best].xmin = int_min(cv->dirty[best].xmin, cv->dirty[n].xmin);
    cv->dirty[best].ymin = int_min(cv->dirty[best].ymin, cv->dirty[n].ymin);
    cv->dirty[best].xmax = int_max(cv->dirty[best].xmax, cv->dirty[n].xmax);
    cv->dirty[best].ymax = int_max(cv->dirty[best].ymax, cv->dirty[n].ymax);

    memmove(&cv->dirty[n], &cv->dirty[n + 1],
            (cv->ndirty - n) * sizeof(cv->dirty[0]));
```
assign values to cv->dirty[best] while best is still -1. so xmax will end up overwriting ndirty and memmove may copy more bytes than the buffers can hold, which can cause a segfault or even more undefined behaviour. If user input gets there, that might become a security issue.


here's an example that makes this happen:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <caca.h>

int main(void)
{
    caca_canvas_t *cv;
    cv = caca_create_canvas(0x50, 0);
    caca_set_canvas_size(cv, 0x50, 0x10000000);
    caca_free_canvas(cv);
    return 0;
}
```

description:
YAML::Syck versions before 1.38 for Perl  has an out-of-bounds read.

The base60 (sexagesimal) parsing code in perl_syck.h has a buffer underflow bug in both int#base60 and float#base60 handlers. When processing the leftmost segment of a colon-separated value (e.g., the 1 in 1:30:45), the inner while loop can decrement a pointer past the start of the string buffer:

    while ( colon >= ptr && *colon != ':' )
    {
        colon--;
    }
    if ( *colon == ':' ) *colon = '\0';  // colon may be ptr-1 here

When no colon is found (final/leftmost segment), colon becomes ptr-1, and the subsequent *colon dereference reads one byte before the allocated buffer.

