issue_list.body:
Hi, I ran a libFuzzer + ASAN/UBSan campaign against the descriptor parsers in `libusb/descriptor.c` (HEAD 77503a79) and found two issues. Both are reachable from a malformed config descriptor returned by an attached USB device, with no application-side input required.

Filing them together because they're in the same file and the same fuzz harness rediscovers both. Same general shape as #1039, but in the cross-platform parser rather than the Windows backend.

Tested on macOS arm64 with Homebrew clang 22.1.1; the parser code is platform-independent so this hits all backends.


---

## Bug A: `parse_interface` leaves `endpoint = NULL` with `bNumEndpoints > 0`

**File:** `libusb/descriptor.c`, around line 245.

In the inner "skip extra/class descriptors" loop:

```c
} else if (header->bLength > size) {
    usbi_warn(ctx, "short extra intf desc read %d/%u",
              size, header->bLength);
    return parsed;          // <-- bug
}
```

By the time this returns:

- `usb_interface->num_altsetting++` has already happened (line 221)
- `ifp->bNumEndpoints` was filled from `buffer[4]` (line 197)
- `ifp->endpoint` is still `NULL` (the `calloc` near line 284 never runs)

So a caller of `libusb_get_active_config_descriptor` / `libusb_get_config_descriptor` gets back a struct with `bNumEndpoints > 0` and `endpoint == NULL`. `libusb.h` documents `endpoint` as:

> Array of endpoint descriptors. This length of this array is determined by the bNumEndpoints field.

`examples/testlibusb.c` and `examples/xusb.c` follow this contract and would crash here.

### Reproducer (20 bytes of descriptor data)

```
09 02 19 00 01 01 00 a0 00     // config:    bNumInterfaces=1
09 04 00 00 01 ff 00 00 00     // interface: bNumEndpoints=1
07 05                          // truncated extra descriptor (claims bLength=7)
```

### Verifier (uses libusb's own descriptor.c source, no copy)

`verify.c` (also attached):

```c
#define ENABLE_LOGGING 0
#include "config.h"
#include "libusbi.h"
#include "descriptor.c"

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

const struct usbi_os_backend usbi_backend = {0};
void usbi_log(struct libusb_context *c, enum libusb_log_level l,
              const char *f, const char *m, ...) { (void)c;(void)l;(void)f;(void)m; }
int libusb_control_transfer(libusb_device_handle *h, uint8_t a, uint8_t b,
                            uint16_t c, uint16_t d, unsigned char *e,
                            uint16_t f, unsigned int g) {
    (void)h;(void)a;(void)b;(void)c;(void)d;(void)e;(void)f;(void)g; return -99;
}

int main(void) {
    uint8_t in[20] = {
        0x09,0x02,0x19,0,0x01,0x01,0,0xa0,0,
        0x09,0x04,0,0,0x01,0xff,0,0,0,
        0x07,0x05
    };
    struct libusb_config_descriptor cfg = {0};
    parse_configuration(NULL, &cfg, in, sizeof(in));
    const struct libusb_interface_descriptor *alt =
        &cfg.interface[0].altsetting[0];
    printf("bNumEndpoints=%u  endpoint=%p\n",
           alt->bNumEndpoints, (void*)alt->endpoint);
    return 0;
}
```

Build and run from the repo root:

```sh
$ ./autogen.sh && make -C libusb
$ clang -O0 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined \
        -I . -I libusb -I libusb/os -DPLATFORM_POSIX=1 \
        -Wno-macro-redefined verify.c -o verify
$ ./verify
bNumEndpoints=1  endpoint=0x0
```

A real application's endpoint loop after this would SEGV.

### Suggested fix

```diff
 } else if (header->bLength > size) {
     usbi_warn(ctx, "short extra intf desc read %d/%u",
               size, header->bLength);
+    ifp->bNumEndpoints = 0;
     return parsed;
 }
```

Just keep the invariant: `bNumEndpoints > 0` implies `endpoint != NULL`.

---

## Bug B: `parse_iad_array` reads one byte past the buffer

**File:** `libusb/descriptor.c`, around line 1381.

```c
while (consumed < size) {
    header.bLength = buf[0];
    header.bDescriptorType = buf[1];
    if (header.bLength < DESC_HEADER_LENGTH) return LIBUSB_ERROR_IO;
    else if (header.bLength > size)               // <-- should be size - consumed
        return LIBUSB_ERROR_IO;
    ...
    buf += header.bLength;
    consumed += header.bLength;
}
```

The bound check uses the original `size` instead of remaining (`size - consumed`). With a single descriptor whose `bLength = size - 1`, `consumed` becomes `size - 1`; the loop re-enters and `buf[1]` is one byte past the malloc allocation.

`parse_configuration` and `parse_interface` in the same file already do this correctly (they decrement `size` and check `bLength > size`). Looks like `parse_iad_array` was written without the same convention.

### Reproducer (9 bytes)

```
08 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 ff
```

### ASAN output (driving `parse_iad_array` from the same verify harness)

```
==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow
READ of size 1 at 0x... (0 bytes after 9-byte region)
    #0 parse_iad_array descriptor.c:1381
```

Reachable via `libusb_get_active_interface_association_descriptors` / `libusb_get_interface_association_descriptors`. 1 byte OOB read, so usually silent on stock allocators but a DoS under hardened ones (GuardMalloc, ASAN deployments, etc.).

### Suggested fix

```diff
-        else if (header.bLength > size) {
+        else if (header.bLength > size - consumed) {
             usbi_warn(ctx, "short config descriptor read %d/%u",
-                      size, header.bLength);
+                      size - consumed, header.bLength);
             return LIBUSB_ERROR_IO;
         }
```

---

Both fixes are 1 line each. I have a PR ready that includes both fixes plus a new `tests/fuzz/fuzz_ss_ep_companion.c` harness for OSS-Fuzz coverage (the same harness that surfaces both bugs). Happy to open it whenever you've had a chance to confirm.

----
The attachments:

[fuzz_ss_ep_companion.c](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/27081613/fuzz_ss_ep_companion.c)
[verify.c](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/27081614/verify.c)

1. **`verify.c`** ：the unity-include verifier referenced in the body. Reproduces both bugs against libusb's actual `descriptor.c` source.
2. **`fuzz_ss_ep_companion.c`**： the single libFuzzer harness that surfaces both bugs (Bug A in ~1 second; Bug B from a 9-byte minimized input on the same corpus).

