Reading the diff at `static/boot.js:445-451` against `origin/master`, plus the click handler at `static/boot.js:441-466` and `static/boot.js:644-656`, I can confirm the regression Greptile flagged is real and worth fixing before merge.

## Code reference

The new default (line 451):

```js
const _micForceMediaRecorderStored=localStorage.getItem(_micForceMediaRecorderKey);
let _forceMediaRecorder=!SpeechRecognition||(_micForceMediaRecorderStored===null?true:_micForceMediaRecorderStored==='1');
```

When the key is absent this unconditionally sets `_forceMediaRecorder=true`. That feeds line 466:

```js
let recognition=(!_forceMediaRecorder&&SpeechRecognition)?new SpeechRecognition():null;
```

so `recognition` becomes `null`. Then on click, the speech branch is skipped and we hit `static/boot.js:653-656`:

```js
if(!_canRecordAudio){
  _isRecording=false;
  showToast(t('mic_network'));
  return;
}
```

## Diagnosis

In a browser/context where `SpeechRecognition` is available but `_canRecordAudio` is false (`navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia` + `window.MediaRecorder`, gated at line 441), the old code fell back to working browser STT. The new default forces the MediaRecorder path, `recognition` is nulled, and the user gets the opaque `mic_network` toast on a mic that previously worked. The guard at line 442 (`if(!SpeechRecognition&&!_canRecordAudio) return;`) only hides the button when *both* are missing, so this case still shows an active-looking button.

## Recommendation

Make the absent-key default prefer server-side STT only when recording is actually supported, otherwise keep browser STT. One-line change at `static/boot.js:451`:

```js
let _forceMediaRecorder=!SpeechRecognition||(_micForceMediaRecorderStored===null?_canRecordAudio:_micForceMediaRecorderStored==='1');
```

`_canRecordAudio` is already in scope from line 441. This keeps the PR's intent (prefer the configured Hermes STT provider by default) without breaking SpeechRecognition-only environments.

## Secondary note on the test

`tests/test_sprint20.py::test_boot_js_prefers_server_side_stt_by_default` asserts the exact minified string `"_micForceMediaRecorderStored===null?true:"`. If you adopt the `_canRecordAudio` guard, that assertion needs updating to `===null?_canRecordAudio:`. More broadly, anchoring on verbatim minified substrings is brittle — any reformat of `boot.js` will break it. Consider asserting the variable name and a looser intent marker rather than the full ternary.

I did not execute any code from the branch; this is a read-only review of the diff and surrounding handler.

Read the diff against `static/panels.js:724-765` (master), the two control handlers at `panels.js:567` (`toggleCronRunExpanded`) and `panels.js:547-556` (the `_cronExpand*` helpers), the row markup at `panels.js:709-716`, and the CSS at `static/style.css:4384-4390`. The toggle-to-collapse idea is good, but the collapse branch reaches into state that belongs to a *different* control. greptile caught half of it; there's a second, related corruption too.

## Two orthogonal pieces of state are being conflated

The cron run row has two independent controls:

- **Open/closed** — driven by `.detail-run-head` onclick → `_loadRunContent(...)` (`panels.js:709`). CSS: `.detail-run-item.open .detail-run-body{display:block;}` (`style.css:4389`). This is the only thing `_loadRunContent` should own.
- **Snippet vs full-output** — driven by the separate `.detail-expand-toggle` button, which calls `event.stopPropagation();toggleCronRunExpanded(...)` (`panels.js:712`). CSS: `.detail-run-body.expanded{max-height:none}` (`style.css:4390`). Its persisted preference is the `_cronRunExpandKey` localStorage value, and the `▴/▾` icon + "Collapse/Expand output" label reflect *that* preference, not open/closed.

The new collapse branch writes to all three pieces of the *second* control while handling the *first*:

```js
item.classList.remove('open');
body.classList.remove('expanded');
_cronExpansionSet(_cronRunExpandKey(jobId, filename), false);   // (1) destroys preference
const btn = item ? item.querySelector('.detail-expand-toggle') : null;
if (btn) { btn.textContent = '▾'; btn.title = (t('cron_expand_output')...); ... }  // (2) corrupts button
```

## Bug 1 (greptile): collapsing resets the full-output preference

`_cronExpansionSet(..., false)` overwrites the localStorage key that `toggleCronRunExpanded` and the "View full output" button (`panels.js:761`) both persist. A user who expanded a run to full output, then closes the row via the header and reopens it, silently gets snippet mode back. The open/close action shouldn't mutate the content-detail preference at all.

## Bug 2: the expand-toggle button is forced to a state that contradicts its real meaning

Resetting `btn.textContent='▾'` / label "Expand output" makes the snippet/full toggle *display* as "currently snippet, click to expand" — but that button governs `.expanded`, which is unrelated to whether the row is open. After this runs, a user who had full-output preference sees the toggle claim snippet mode even though their stored preference (now also clobbered by bug 1) no longer matches what the button would do on next open. The two bugs compound.

## Suggested fix — collapse should only touch `.open`

The header click governs exactly one thing. Drop the preference write and the button mutation entirely:

```js
if (item.classList.contains('open')) {
  // Toggle: header click closes an open row. Leave the snippet/full
  // preference (_cronRunExpandKey) and the .detail-expand-toggle button
  // untouched — those belong to toggleCronRunExpanded, not this path.
  item.classList.remove('open');
  return;
}
item.classList.add('open');
```

Since `.detail-run-body` is `display:none` while the item lacks `.open` (`style.css:4388-4389`), you don't even need to strip `.expanded` — it's not visible when closed, and preserving it means the row re-opens in the user's chosen mode. That also keeps the perf win you're after (no re-fetch on collapse) without the state loss.

A small regression test (or a manual note in the PR) covering "expand to full → close via header → reopen → still full output" would lock this down.

